<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Aldus Manutius</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1450–<a href="/years/1515">1515</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> · Also known as Aldo Manuzio</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Aldus Manutius was an Italian humanist and printer who founded the Aldine Press in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> in the 1490s. He published the first printed editions of many Greek classics, introduced italic type and the small portable octavo book, and helped make Venice the leading centre of European <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/printing-press">printing</a>. He had earlier been tutor to the princes of Carpi, Alberto and Lionello Pio, who supported his press.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/aldus-manutius.jpg" alt="Aldus Manutius" width="320" height="445" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Rota, Martinus Sibenicensis (1520?-1583). Graveur · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Portrait_d%27Alde_Manuce,_en_buste,_de_profil_dirig%C3%A9_%C3%A0_droite_-_estampe_-_btv1b84007044_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Aldus Manutius was born at Bassiano, near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, between 1449 and 1452, into a wealthy family, and was sent to Rome to become a humanist scholar. He studied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> there under Gaspare da Verona and attended lectures by Domizio Calderini in the early 1470s. From 1475 to 1478 he studied Greek at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ferrara">Ferrara</a> under Battista Guarino. In 1482 he went to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mirandola">Mirandola</a> with his friend <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/giovanni-pico-della-mirandola">Giovanni Pico della Mirandola</a>, who recommended him as tutor to his nephews Alberto and Leonello Pio, princes of Carpi. The families of Pico and Alberto Pio paid the first costs of his press and gave him lands in Carpi. He decided that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> was the best place for his work and settled there in 1490.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> he met the printer Andrea Torresano, who became his lifelong partner and printed his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> grammar, the Institutiones grammaticae, on 9 March 1493. The Aldine Press, established in 1494, issued its first book in March 1495, Constantine Lascaris's Erotemata. Its first great achievement was a five-volume folio <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aristotle">Aristotle</a>, begun in 1495 and completed in 1497 and 1498, and it printed nine comedies of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aristophanes">Aristophanes</a> in 1498. Manutius wanted to give readers Greek works in their original form. He commissioned Greek and Latin types resembling humanist handwriting, the first known precursor of italic type, and introduced small portable books, the enchiridia, a predecessor of the paperback.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/erasmus">Erasmus</a> asked him to publish his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> translations of Hecuba and Iphigenia in Aulis; Iphigenia in Aulis appeared in December 1507. Erasmus then went to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a>, spent ten months at the press and lived in the house of Manutius and Torresano, and there expanded his collection of proverbs from 819 to 3,260 entries, published as the Adagiorum Chiliades in 1508. Wars interrupted the press, but in 1513 Manutius issued a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plato">Plato</a> dedicated to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-leo-x">Pope Leo X</a>. In 1505 he married Maria, Torresano's daughter, which combined the partners' shares.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Manutius wrote his will on 16 January 1515 and died on 6 February. Torresano and his two sons carried on the business, and Manutius's son Paulus, born in 1512, later took it over, leading it from 1539 with his brothers under the imprint Sons of Aldus until his death in 1574. A Greek Septuagint he had begun appeared in 1518, the first printed. Paul F. Grendler wrote that Manutius ensured the survival of a large number of ancient texts and greatly eased the spread of Italian Renaissance humanism to the rest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Aldus Manutius” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Industry flourished: shipbuilding at the Arsenal, glass-making, concentrated on Murano from 1291, silk weaving, soap, and from 1469 printing, with <b>Aldus Manutius</b> making Venice the leading centre of the European book trade around 1500.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-venice">Republic of Venice · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>…began teaching in Florence in 1397; Greek scholars brought manuscripts to Italy before and after the fall of Constantinople in 1453; the printer <b>Aldus Manutius</b> published the Greek classics in Venice; and Erasmus published the Greek New Testament in 1516, an event of great importance for the Reformation.</blockquote><a href="/language/ancient-greek">Ancient Greek · Change &amp; Descendants</a></li><li><blockquote>There, from 1495, <b>Aldus Manutius</b> issued the first printed editions in Greek of Aristotle, Aristophanes, Thucydides, Sophocles, Herodotus and Plato, and from 1501 small portable classics in a new italic type.</blockquote><a href="/movement/renaissance-humanism">Renaissance humanism · Course of Events</a></li><li><blockquote>Alberto III Pio, pupil and patron of the printer <b>Aldus Manutius</b>, rebuilt Carpi around a huge new square with the castle, a new cathedral and porticoes.</blockquote><a href="/state/lordship-of-carpi">Lordship of Carpi</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Bassiano, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=41.55,13.03&amp;year=1492" aria-label="Bassiano on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4868 683 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Aldus Manutius"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5318 953h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5318 953h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=41.55,13.03&amp;year=1492">What was here in 1492? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
